From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Eugene Feinberg <uge@persimmons.ai>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 16:55:23 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 02/14] scl: fix Scl_Con directive macros (dead new-format
 parser)

SCL_DIRECTIVE/SCL_DEF_DIRECTIVE stringified the macro ARGUMENT NAME
(# suppresses expansion), so the parser matched literal
".default_SCL_INPUT_ARR" and no documented directive ever parsed.
ITEM is already a string macro; plain literal concatenation is what
was intended. With this and the read_constr -n flag fix, boundary
budgets actually reach &nf and the (new) sizing STA.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/map/scl/sclCon.h | 10 ++++++++--
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/map/scl/sclCon.h b/src/map/scl/sclCon.h
index fe7776c12..cc7735a5e 100644
--- a/src/map/scl/sclCon.h
+++ b/src/map/scl/sclCon.h
@@ -56,8 +56,14 @@ struct Scl_Con_t_
 #define SCL_OUTPUT_REQ   "output_required"
 #define SCL_OUTPUT_LOAD  "output_load"
 
-#define SCL_DIRECTIVE(ITEM)     "."#ITEM
-#define SCL_DEF_DIRECTIVE(ITEM) ".default_"#ITEM
+// ITEM is itself a string macro (SCL_INPUT_ARR = "input_arrival" etc.), so
+// plain literal concatenation is what is wanted. The original "."#ITEM
+// stringified the ARGUMENT NAME (# suppresses macro expansion), so the
+// parser matched ".default_SCL_INPUT_ARR" -- no documented directive ever
+// parsed, making the whole new-format reader dead code (together with the
+// read_constr -n flag bug fixed in scl.c).
+#define SCL_DIRECTIVE(ITEM)     "." ITEM
+#define SCL_DEF_DIRECTIVE(ITEM) ".default_" ITEM
 
 #define SCL_NUM          1000
 #define SCL_INFINITY    (0x3FFFFFFF)
-- 
